EXT. ROADSIDE DRIVE-IN THEATRE - NIGHT

Rain on a field of parked cars. A giant screen, torn at one corner, shows a black-and-white kiss in forty-foot faces. Speakers hang on posts like mourners. Windshield wipers keep time.

At the back of the lot, under the sagging eaves of the projection booth, ANNELIESE VOSKUIJLEN, 30s, a bride in a rain-soaked gown and borrowed denim jacket, stands with a lit cigarette she is not smoking. Her sister-in-law-to-be CORINNE VASQUEZ-LEE, 30s, a maid of honor with a ruined updo, holds a stolen bottle of champagne by the neck.

Between them, the rain. Between them, everything.

CORINNE
You can't just walk out of your own wedding.

ANNELIESE
I walked out of my own wedding.

CORINNE
The caterer is crying.

ANNELIESE
The caterer is always crying.

Corinne laughs, wet, despite herself. Hands over the bottle. Anneliese drinks. Hands it back.

ANNELIESE (CONT'D)
He's good, Corinne.

CORINNE
He's wonderful.

ANNELIESE
So why does it feel like a cage?

CORINNE
(gently)
Maybe because it's a cage you picked.

A silhouette appears at the edge of the lot, umbrella tilted. GABRIEL ACHTERBERG, 30s, a groom in a ruined waistcoat, jogs toward them through the puddles, the umbrella useless.

GABRIEL
(calling)
Anna! Anna, I'm not here to --

ANNELIESE
Gabe.

CORINNE
I'll be in the car.

Corinne retreats, taking the champagne. Gabriel arrives, drenched, breathless. He stops three feet away.

GABRIEL
Your mother is giving a speech to the empty chairs.

ANNELIESE
That sounds like her.

GABRIEL
I told her you needed air.

ANNELIESE
I needed this.

She gestures at the lot: the rain, the cars, the screen. On it, the silent couple kiss again.

GABRIEL
I never knew you liked the pictures.

ANNELIESE
I came here with my father. Every Friday. Until he sold the Chevy.

GABRIEL
You never told me.

ANNELIESE
You never asked.

The sting lands. He takes it, nods.

GABRIEL
Fair.

TAKESHI NAKAMURA, 70s, the projectionist, leans out of the booth window with a thermos and a skeptical eyebrow.

TAKESHI
Folks, you are blocking the exit.

ANNELIESE
Sorry.

GABRIEL ^
Sorry!

TAKESHI
You two the runaway wedding?

ANNELIESE
Probably.

TAKESHI
Good. Come in. Better view from up here.

Gabriel and Anneliese look at each other, uncertain, then climb the iron stair into the booth. It smells of warm dust and film. A single projector clatters in the dark, throwing a beam through a square window.

The old man pours three paper cups from the thermos.

TAKESHI (CONT'D)
My wife and I watched this one thirty-one years ago. She said, "He's going to leave her."

GABRIEL
Does he?

TAKESHI
Watch.

On the screen, the man walks into the rain. The woman does not call after him. The camera holds on her face.

Anneliese reaches for Gabriel's hand, does not take it, rests her fingers an inch from his.

ANNELIESE
(quietly)
She's going to let him.

TAKESHI
Only in this version.

The reel clatters. The picture jumps. In the splice, a frame goes white and then, impossibly, the man turns around.

Gabriel's breath catches. Anneliese's hand closes over his.

GABRIEL
Is that...

TAKESHI
Different print. Spliced it myself, the winter she died. I couldn't stand the ending either.

Rain on the roof. Three paper cups. The woman on the screen takes one step toward the man.

ANNELIESE
(whispering)
Takeshi. You're a terrible projectionist.

TAKESHI
And you are a terrible bride.

Gabriel starts to laugh. Then Anneliese. The old man joins them, wheezing, coughing, shaking his head.

Outside, in the car, Corinne watches the window of the booth glow like a lantern and finishes the champagne alone.
